\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Disorder lines, cyclic centers and electric–magnetic comparison}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{The [affine Wilson formula](fdip-00NP) replaces a Wilson
current by a shift of dual weights. The shift represents a
spanning sheet, while its coboundary #{-D_1J} is a
#{(n-1)}-cochain dual to the physical one-dimensional
current #{J}. The physical defect is a line in every
#{n}; its dimension is distinct from its cochain degree.
For #{n=3} this is a frustrated spin sheet ending on that
line. For #{n=4}, where the dual variable is a one-form
gauge field, it has the conventional disorder or 't Hooft-loop
interpretation. Wegner's changed-coupling discussion is in
\citet{Section 7.1, pp. 15–16, Equations (66)–(68)}{wegner2014duality}.}

\p{Wegner's cyclic convention is
#{\omega_m^*=N^{-1/2}\sum_xe^{2\pi imx/N}\omega_x}
in \citet{Section 6, p. 13, Equation (59)}{wegner2014duality}.
For the complex specialization #{\zeta=e^{2\pi i/N}},
our negative-exponent average is exactly
##{\widehat w(m)=N^{-1/2}\omega_{-m}^*.}
This comparison uses both the index reversal and the
square-root normalization; the ring theorem itself needs
only the inverse of #{N}. Wegner's twisted-sector formula
in \citet{Section 4.2, p. 9, Equation (39)}{wegner2014duality}
concerns its two-dimensional Ising torus. The arbitrary-ring
and higher-dimensional sector factors here are those proved
in \ref{fdip-00NO}.}

\p{The complex characters identify the Pontryagin dual
#{\widehat A=\operatorname{Hom}(A,U(1))} with #{A}
after a cyclic character choice. Mathlib's
\leanref{AddChar.zmodAddEquiv} supplies this cyclic character
correspondence. It concerns the unit-circle character group,
not every element of #{\operatorname{Hom}(A,R^\times)};
for #{R=\mathbb C\times\mathbb C}, the latter has
#{N^2} elements.}

\p{The center of #{SU(N)} is the cyclic group
#{\{e^{2\pi ik/N}I: k\in\mathbb Z/N\}}, so the
[neutral SU(N) example](fdip-00ND) and the finite cyclic
identity share this group-theoretic comparison.
Kapustin and Witten discuss finite-center pairings and
electric and magnetic fluxes in
\citet{Section 7.1, pp. 103–106, Equations (7.1)–(7.6)}{kapustin2006electric}.
These pairings provide a finite Abelian analogy for the
exchange of characters and flux sectors. Their global-form
table identifies the Langlands dual of #{SU(N)} as
#{PSU(N)}; see \citet{Table 1, p. 3}{kapustin2006electric}.
The center #{\mathbb Z_N} is not that dual group.
The finite proof does not reduce an #{SU(N)} gauge model
to its center or establish non-Abelian lattice duality or
supersymmetric S-duality.}
